== Chapter XV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[Decays in degrees of grace caused by indwelling sin. The ways it prevails for this purpose.]
]

#smallcaps[The] ways and means by which #emph[indwelling sin] prevails on believers to bring them to habitual #emph[declines] and decays in #emph[degrees of grace] and holiness now come under consideration, and they are many.

#emph[First,] at the #emph[first conversion] and calling of #emph[sinners] to God and Christ, many #emph[fresh springs] usually break out in their souls, and refreshing showers come on them, which carry them up to a #emph[high level] of faith, love, holiness, fruitfulness, and obedience. It is like a river in a #emph[flood,] when many smaller streams run into it: it swells over its banks and rolls on with more than its ordinary fullness. Now if these #emph[springs] are not kept open, if they do not prevail for these showers to continue, they must decay and go backward. We will name #emph[one or two] of them.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, they have #emph[a fresh, vigorous sense of pardoning mercy.] As this is in the soul, so will its love and delight in God be, and so will its obedience be. As the sense of gospel pardon is, I say, so will the #emph[life] of gospel love be. Luke 7:47: “Therefore I tell you,” says our Savior about the poor woman, “because her many sins have been forgiven, she has loved much. But he who has been forgiven little loves little.” Her #emph[great love] was an evidence of #emph[great forgiveness,] and of her great sense of it. For our Savior is not giving a reason for her #emph[forgiveness,] as though it were for her love, but a reason for her #emph[love]: that it came from her forgiveness. In the preceding #emph[parable,] from verse 38 onward, he had convinced the #emph[Pharisees] he was dealing with that the one who was #emph[forgiven most] would love most (#emph[verse] 43). From this he gives an account of the #emph[great love] of the woman, springing from the sense she had of the #emph[great forgiveness] she had so freely received. So sinners at their first conversion are very aware of great forgiveness; “of whom I am the worst” lies closest to their heart. This greatly subdues their hearts and spirits to all that is in God, and makes them alive to all obedience: that such poor cursed sinners as they were should be so #emph[freely] delivered and pardoned! The love of God and of Christ in their #emph[forgiveness] powerfully conquers and constrains them to make it their business to #emph[live for God.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] the #emph[fresh taste they have had of spiritual things] keeps up such a savor and relish of them in their souls that the worldly satisfactions by which men are drawn away from close walking with God become tasteless and undesirable to them. Having tasted the #emph[wine] of the gospel, they desire no other, for they say, “this is better.” So it was with the apostles when the #emph[choice] of departing from Christ was offered them, after many false professors had fallen away: “Do you#super[+] want to leave too?” (John 6:67). They answer through #emph[Peter,] “Lord, to whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life” (verse 68). They had such a #emph[fresh savor] and relish of the doctrine of the gospel and the grace of Christ on their souls that they could not entertain any thought of turning away from it. Think of a man who has been kept long in a #emph[dungeon.] If he is suddenly brought out into the light of the sun, he finds so much pleasure and satisfaction in it, in the beauties of the old creation, that he thinks he can never tire of it, and that he will never on any account be content to be in darkness again. So it is with souls when they are first brought into the “marvelous light” of Christ, to behold the beauties of the #emph[new creation.] They see a new #emph[glory] in him that has quite tarnished the desirableness of all earthly pastimes. And they see a new guilt and filth in sin that gives them an utter abhorrence of its old delights and pleasures; and so with other things.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Now while these and #emph[similar springs] are kept open in the souls of converted sinners, they constrain them to a vigorous, active holiness. They can never #emph[do enough for God.] So their zeal #emph[as saints] often does not let them escape without some blots on their prudence #emph[as men,] as might be shown in many of the #emph[martyrs] of old.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This, then, is the #emph[first] way, or at least #emph[one way,] in which indwelling sin prepares men for #emph[decays] and declines in grace and obedience: it tries to #emph[stop up] or taint #emph[these springs.] And there are several ways in which it brings this about.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] it works by #emph[sloth] and #emph[negligence.] It prevails on the soul to neglect #emph[stirring up] continual thoughts of, or about, the things that so powerfully #emph[influence] it to strict and fruitful obedience. If care is not taken, if diligence and watchfulness are not used, and all the means God has appointed are not observed to keep a quick and living sense of these things on the soul, they will dry up and decay. As a result, the obedience that should spring from them will do so too. #emph[Isaac dug wells,] but the #emph[Philistines] stopped them up, and his flocks had no benefit from them. Let the heart fall out of the habit of gracious, soul-moving thoughts of the love of God, the cross of Christ, the greatness and excellence of gospel mercy, and the beauty of holiness, even a little, and these things will quickly be as much #emph[estranged] from a man as he can be from them. He who shuts his eyes in the sun for a while can see nothing at all when he opens them again. And as much #emph[faith] as a man loses toward these things, so much #emph[power] will they lose toward him. Because of his unbelief they can do little or nothing on him, though once they were so exceedingly effectual toward him. So it was with the #emph[bride] in the #emph[Song of Songs] (ch. 5:2). Christ calls to her (v. 1) with a wonderfully #emph[loving] and gracious invitation to communion with himself. She had once been #emph[enraptured] at the first hearing of that “joyful sound.” But now, under the power of #emph[sloth] and carnal ease, she returns a sorry, #emph[excusing] answer to his call, which ended in her own notable loss and sorrow. #emph[Indwelling sin,] I say, prevails on the souls of men by #emph[spiritual sloth,] so that they fail to heed the motions of God’s Spirit in their former apprehensions of divine love, and neglect stirring up continual thoughts of #emph[faith] about it. When it does, a #emph[decay] grows on the whole soul without their noticing it. So God often complains that his people had “forgotten him”\; that is, they grew unmindful of his love and grace, and this was the beginning of their #emph[apostasy.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it works by #emph[unsettling the soul,] so that it has formal, weary, #emph[powerless] thoughts of the things that should prevail on it to #emph[diligence] in thankful obedience. The apostle warns us that in dealing with God we should “worship God acceptably with reverence and awe,” because of his purity, holiness, and majesty (Heb 12:28, 29). And this is what the Lord himself spoke at the destruction of #emph[Nadab] and #emph[Abihu]: “To those who come near Me I will show My holiness” (Lev 10:3). He will be dealt with in an awe-filled, #emph[holy,] reverent manner. So we are to deal with all the things of God in which, or by which, we have communion with him. The soul is to have a great reverence for God in them. When men begin to treat them with #emph[careless] and #emph[ordinary thoughts,] and do not use them and make the most of them for the ends for which they are appointed, these things lose all their beauty and glory and power toward them. When we have anything to do in which faith and love toward God are to be exercised, we must do it with all our hearts, with all our minds, strength, and souls, not carelessly and #emph[perfunctorily,] which God abhors. He requires not only that we keep his love and grace in remembrance, but that, as far as lies in us, we do it according to their worth and excellence. It was the sin of #emph[Hezekiah] that he “did not repay the favor shown to him” (2 Chr 32:25). So while we consider gospel truths, the utmost effort of the soul ought to be that we may be transformed into the same image or likeness (2 Cor 3:18); that is, that they may have their full power and effect on us. Otherwise #emph[James] tells us what our “beholding the glory of the Lord as in a mirror,” which the apostle mentions there, comes to; that is, our reading or hearing the mind of God in Christ revealed in the gospel (ch. 1:23, 24). “It is like a man who looks at his face in a mirror, and after observing himself goes away and immediately forgets what he looks like.” It makes no impression on him; it forms no #emph[idea] or image of his likeness in his imagination, because he does it only #emph[carelessly,] with a passing look. So it is with men who will indeed think of #emph[gospel truths,] but in a careless way, without striving with all their hearts, minds, and strength to have them grafted into their souls, and all their effects produced in them. Now this is how #emph[sinners] deal with these things in their first #emph[engagements] with God. They never think of #emph[pardoning mercy] without laboring to move their whole souls with it, and stirring themselves up to fitting affections and to returns of constant obedience. They never think of the #emph[excellence of Christ,] and of spiritual things now newly revealed to them in a saving light, without pressing with all their might after a further and fuller enjoyment of them. This keeps them #emph[humble] and holy; this makes them thankful and fruitful. But if the utmost #emph[diligence] and care are not used to make use of this wisdom and grow in it, and to keep up this frame, #emph[indwelling sin,] working by the #emph[vanity of men’s minds,] will bring them without their noticing it to content themselves with #emph[careless] and #emph[rare] thoughts of these things, without a diligent, earnest effort to give these things their due effect on the soul. As men #emph[decay] in this, so they will assuredly #emph[decay] and decline in the #emph[power of holiness] and close walking with God. When the #emph[springs] are stopped up or tainted, the streams will not run so #emph[swiftly,] or at least not so #emph[sweetly,] as before. By this means some, under an unbroken profession, wither almost to nothing without noticing it. They talk of religion and spiritual things as much as they ever did in their lives, and perform #emph[duties] as constantly as they ever did. Yet they have poor, lean, starving souls as to any real and effectual communion with God. By the power and subtlety of #emph[indwelling sin] they have grown #emph[formal,] and learned to deal with spiritual things in a #emph[superficial] way, and by it they have lost all the life, vigor, savor, and efficacy these things had toward them. Be always #emph[serious] in spiritual things, if you ever intend to be made better by them.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly, indwelling sin] often prevails to stop up these springs of gospel obedience by false and foolish opinions that corrupt the #emph[simplicity] of the gospel. False opinions are #emph[works of the flesh.] For the most part they come from the #emph[vanity] and darkness of men’s minds, with a mixture, more or less, of corrupt affections. The apostle was #emph[jealous] over his #emph[Corinthians] in this matter. He was afraid that their minds might “by any means be led astray from the simplicity that is in Christ” (2 Cor 11:2, 3), which he knew would be followed by a #emph[decay] and decline in faith, love, and obedience. And so things often turn out in this case. We have seen some who received a sweet #emph[taste] of the love of God in Christ, of the excellence of #emph[pardoning mercy,] and walked humbly with God for many years in the faith and understanding of the truth. Yet by the corruption of their minds from the simplicity that is in Christ through false and foolish opinions, they have despised all their own experiences and rejected all the efficacy of truth for furthering their obedience. That is why #emph[John] warns the #emph[elect lady] and her children to take care that they are not led astray, “so that they do not lose what they have worked for” (2 John 8); that is, so that they do not themselves throw away all their former obedience as lost and of no value. We have countless instances of this in the days we live in. How many are there who not many years ago set an unspeakable value #emph[on the pardon of sin in the blood of Christ,] who delighted in the gospel’s revelations of spiritual things and walked in obedience to God because of them? And now, having been #emph[deceived] and turned aside from the truth as it is in Jesus, they despise these #emph[springs] of their own former obedience. And as this is done #emph[grossly] and openly in some, so there are more #emph[secret] and more #emph[plausible] suggestions of corrupt opinions that taint the springs and fountains of gospel obedience. They gain ground on men through the vanity of their minds, which is a principal part of indwelling sin. Such are all opinions that tend to lessen #emph[special grace] in its freedom and efficacy, and to exalt the wills or efforts of men in their spiritual power and ability. They are all #emph[works of the flesh.] However much some may claim that they are useful for #emph[promoting holiness,] they will be found to #emph[taint the springs] of true evangelical obedience, to turn the heart from God without its noticing, and to bring the whole soul into a spiritual #emph[decay.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[This is one way in which #emph[indwelling sin] produces this destructive effect. It draws men away from the power, purity, and fruitfulness that went with their first conversion and engagements to God, and brings them into #emph[habitual decline,] at least in the #emph[degrees] of their holiness and grace. There is nothing we ought to watch against more, if we intend to deal effectually with this powerful and subtle enemy. It is no small part of the #emph[wisdom of faith] to observe whether gospel truths still have the same #emph[savor] to the soul and the same #emph[efficacy] on it as they once had, and whether an effort is kept up to make use of them continually, as at the first. A commandment that is always practiced is always new, as #emph[John] says of the commandment of love. And to the man who really makes use of gospel truths, they will always be #emph[new] and fresh, though he hears them a thousand times, because they move him to #emph[newness] of practice. But to another man, who grows #emph[accustomed] to them, they are burdensome and #emph[ordinary]\; he even loathes the #emph[manna] he is so used to.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly, indwelling sin] does this #emph[by taking men off their guard against the return of Satan.] When our Lord Christ first comes to take possession of any soul for himself, he binds that “strong man and plunders his goods.” He strips him of all his power, dominion, and interest. Satan, dispossessed in this way and frustrated in his hopes and expectations, leaves the soul, finding it newly dead to his baits. So he left our Savior after his first fruitless attempts. But it is said that “he left Him until an opportune time” (Luke 4:13). He intended to return again when he saw his advantage. So it is with #emph[believers] too. Having been cast out of his interest in them, he leaves them for a #emph[time,] or at least does so comparatively. Freed from his assaults and perplexing temptations, they go on #emph[vigorously] in the course of their obedience, and so flourish in the ways of God. But this does not last. Satan returns again, and if the soul does not stand continually on #emph[guard] against him, he will quickly gain such advantages as will put a notable interruption on its fruitfulness and obedience. That is why some, after spending #emph[some time,] perhaps #emph[some years,] in cheerful, exemplary walking with God, have spent all their later days, upon Satan’s #emph[return,] wrestling with the perplexing temptations he has entangled them in. Others have plainly fallen under the power of his assaults. It is like a man who has lived #emph[usefully] among his neighbors for a while, doing good and sharing as he was able, giving to the poor and helping all around him. At length he falls into the hands of vexatious, quarrelsome, oppressive men, and is forced to spend all his time and income defending himself against them at law, and so becomes useless in the place where he lives. So it is with many a believer. He has walked in a fruitful course of obedience, to the glory of God and the building up of the church of Christ. Then, being attacked afresh by the #emph[return] of Satan in one way or another, he has enough to do for the rest of his life to keep himself #emph[alive,] while in the meantime he decays woefully and goes backward in many graces. Now this too, though Satan has a hand in it, is from #emph[indwelling sin.] I mean that the success Satan gains in his undertaking comes from it. Indwelling sin encourages him, makes way for his return, and gives his temptations an entrance. You#super[+] know how it is with those from whom he is cast out only by #emph[gospel conviction.] After he has wandered and waited a while, he says, “I will return to the house I left.” And what is the outcome? Carnal lusts have prevailed over the man’s convictions and made his soul fit to entertain #emph[returning devils.] It is the same as to the degree of prevailing power Satan gains against believers, through advantages given him by sin, which leaves the soul exposed to his temptations.]

Now the ways and means by which #emph[indwelling sin] gives Satan an advantage for his #emph[return] are all those that dispose believers toward a #emph[decline,] which will be mentioned afterward. Satan is a diligent, watchful, and crafty adversary. He will neglect no opportunity and no advantage that is offered him. So wherever our spiritual strength is impaired by #emph[sin,] or whichever way our lusts press, Satan joins in with that weakness and presses toward that ruin. All the actings of the law of sin, then, serve this end of Satan. So at present I will mention only one or two of them that seem principally to invite Satan to attempt a #emph[return.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] it entangles the soul in the things of the world, all of which are so many suppliers for Satan. When #emph[Pharaoh] had let the people go, he heard after a while that they were “entangled in the wilderness,” and he supposes that he will therefore now #emph[overtake] them and destroy them. This stirs him up to #emph[pursue] them. Satan finds those he has been cast out from entangled in the things of the world, through which he is sure to find easy #emph[access] to them, and he is encouraged to make a fresh attempt on them, as the #emph[spider] is encouraged to come down on the strongest fly that is #emph[entangled] in its web. For by his temptations he comes only to drive them into what they are already inclined to by their own lusts, adding #emph[poison] to their lusts and #emph[paint] to their #emph[objects.] And by this advantage he often gets so far into the souls of men that they are never properly free of him again as long as they live. As men’s #emph[distractions] from the world increase, so do their #emph[entanglements] from Satan. When they have more to do in the world than they can well manage, they will have more to deal with from Satan than they can well withstand. When men are made spiritually #emph[faint] by dealing in and with the world, Satan attacks them as #emph[Amalek] attacked the faint and weak of the people who came out of #emph[Egypt.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it produces this effect by #emph[making the soul negligent] and taking it off its guard. We have shown at length before that one main part of the #emph[effectual deceitfulness of indwelling sin] is to make the soul #emph[heedless,] and to turn it away from the diligent, watchful attention to its duty that is required. Now there is nothing for which diligence and watchfulness are more strictly commanded than the #emph[returning assaults] of Satan (1 Pet 5:8): “Be sober-minded and alert.” And why so? Because of “your#super[+] adversary the devil.” Unless you#super[+] are exceedingly #emph[watchful,] he will surprise you#super[+] at one time or another. And all the commands of our blessed #emph[Savior] to #emph[watch] are always with reference to Satan and his temptations. Now when the soul is made #emph[careless] and heedless, forgetting what an enemy it has to deal with, or is lifted up with the #emph[successes] it has newly gained against him, then is Satan’s time to attempt to re-enter his old #emph[dwelling.] If he cannot do that, he still makes their lives uncomfortable to themselves and unfruitful to others, weakening their #emph[root] and withering their #emph[fruit] by his poisoning temptations. He comes down on our duties of #emph[obedience] as the birds came down on #emph[Abraham’s] sacrifice. If we do not watch, as Abraham did, to drive them away \[for by resistance he is overcome and put to flight\], he will #emph[devour] them.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, #emph[indwelling sin] takes advantage of the #emph[evil examples of professors] among whom men live to put forth its efficacy and deceit, so as to draw them away from their #emph[early zeal] and holiness, from their #emph[first faith, love,] and works. When men first engage in the ways of God, they have a #emph[reverent esteem] for those they believe received that mercy before them. They love and honor these, as is their duty. But after a while they find many of them walking #emph[unevenly] and #emph[crookedly] in many things, and not unlike the men of the world. Here #emph[sin] does not miss its advantage. Without their noticing it, it prevails on men to go along with them. “This way, this course of walking, does #emph[well enough] for others; why may it not do so for us too?” Such is the inward thought of many, and it works effectually in them. And so, through the craft of #emph[sin,] the generation of professors corrupt one another. Think of a #emph[stream] rising from a clear spring or fountain. While it runs in its own channel and keeps its water unmixed, it preserves its purity and cleanness. But when in its course it meets other streams that are #emph[cloudy] and #emph[foul,] though they run the same way, it becomes #emph[muddy] and #emph[discolored] too. So it is in this case. Believers come out from the spring of the #emph[new birth] with some purity and cleanness, and for a while they keep it in the course of their private walking with God. But then, at times, they come into #emph[company] with #emph[others] whose profession flows and runs the same way as theirs, toward heaven, but who are #emph[muddied] and #emph[stained] with sin and the world. Then they are often corrupted with them and by them, and so decline from their first purity, faith, and holiness. Now in case this has been the case with anyone who reads this discourse, I will add a few cautions that are #emph[necessary] to preserve men from this infection.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[First,] in the #emph[body of professors] there is a great number of #emph[hypocrites.] We cannot say of #emph[this] or #emph[that] man that he is one, yet it is most certain that there are some. Our Savior has told us that it will be so to the end of the world. Not all who have #emph[oil] in their #emph[lamps] have it in their #emph[vessels.] Let men take care how they give themselves up to #emph[conforming] to the professors they meet. Otherwise, instead of #emph[saints] and the best of men, they may sometimes set up #emph[hypocrites,] who are the #emph[worst,] as their example. And when they think they are #emph[like] those who bear the #emph[image of God,] they are conforming themselves to those who bear the #emph[image of Satan.]]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] you do not know what the #emph[present temptations] of those whose #emph[ways] you observe may be. Perhaps they are under some #emph[special desertion] from God, and so are #emph[withering] for a season until he sends them some #emph[refreshing showers] from above. Perhaps they are entangled with some #emph[special corruptions] that are their burden, which you do not know of. For anyone to fall willingly into the kind of frame others are thrown into by the power of their temptations, or to think that what he sees is enough for others, whose diseases he does not know, will be enough for him, is #emph[folly] and presumption. Suppose a man knows #emph[this] or #emph[that] person to be a living man of healthy constitution. If he sees him #emph[crawling] about his affairs, feeble and weak, sometimes falling, sometimes standing, and making little progress in anything, will he think it enough for himself to do the same? Will he not ask whether the person he sees has lately fallen into some #emph[disease] or #emph[sickness] that has weakened him and brought him into that condition? He certainly will. Take care, Christians: many of the professors you#super[+] associate with are sick and wounded. The wounds of some of them “are foul and festering because of their folly.” If you#super[+] have any #emph[spiritual health,] do not think their weak and uneven walking will be accepted from you#super[+]; much less think it will be well for you#super[+] to become #emph[sick] and wounded too.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Thirdly,] remember that of many of the #emph[best Christians] only the #emph[worst] is known and seen. Many who keep up precious communion with God still often do not make so glorious an appearance, because of their #emph[natural temperaments] of freeness or passion, as others who perhaps fall short of them in grace and the power of godliness. As to their outward conduct, it may seem that they are scarcely #emph[saved,] when as to their #emph[faith and love] they may be eminent. Like the #emph[king’s daughter,] they may be “all glorious within,” though their gown is not always “embroidered with gold.” Take care, then, that you#super[+] are not infected with their #emph[worst,] when perhaps you#super[+] are not able to imitate them in their #emph[best.] But to return.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fourthly,] sin does this work by #emph[cherishing some secret particular lust in the heart.] The soul fights against it faintly. It does #emph[fight against] it out of sincerity; it cannot help doing so. But it does not make thorough work of mortifying it vigorously by the strength and power of grace. Now where it is so with the soul, an #emph[habitual decline] in holiness will assuredly follow. #emph[David] shows us how in his #emph[first days] he kept his heart close to God (Ps 18:23): “I have been blameless before Him and kept myself from my iniquity.” His great care was that no #emph[one lust] should prevail in him or over him, which might be called #emph[his iniquity] in a #emph[special way.] #emph[Paul] steered the same course (1 Cor 9:27). He was in #emph[danger of being lifted up] by his spiritual revelations and enjoyments. This makes him “discipline his body and make it his slave,” so that no carnal reasonings or vain imaginations might take hold in him. But where #emph[indwelling sin] has provoked, irritated, and strengthened a #emph[special lust,] it assuredly proves a #emph[principal means] of a general decline. For as an infirmity and weakness in any one vital part will make the whole body consumptive, so the #emph[weakness in any one grace] that a perplexing lust brings with it will make the soul consumptive. It weakens spiritual strength in every way. It weakens #emph[confidence] in God, in faith and prayer. The #emph[knees] will be feeble and the #emph[hands] will hang down in dealing with God where a galling and unmortified lust lies in the heart. It will “take such hold of the soul that it will not be able to look up” (Ps 40:12). It darkens the mind with #emph[countless foolish imaginations,] which it stirs up to make provision for itself. It #emph[galls] the conscience with the spots and stains it brings on the soul in and by its actings. It contends in the #emph[will] for rule and dominion. An #emph[active, stirring corruption] wants the commanding power in the soul, and it is again and again ready to take the throne. It #emph[disturbs] the thoughts, and sometimes it will even frighten the soul away from dealing with it by meditation, for fear that the corrupt affections will be entangled by it, and grace will lose ground instead of prevailing. It often #emph[breaks] out into scandalous sins, as it did in #emph[David] and #emph[Hezekiah,] and loads the sinner with sorrow and discouragement. By these and similar means it becomes like a #emph[moth in a garment] to the soul, eating up and devouring its strongest threads. So although the whole still hangs #emph[loosely] together, it is easily torn to pieces. Though the soul that is in this condition keeps up a #emph[fair profession] for a season, the man’s strength is secretly devoured, and every temptation tears and rips his #emph[conscience] at will. It is with such men as with some who have been of a sound, strong, #emph[athletic] constitution for many years. Some secret, #emph[wasting fever] seizes them. For a time they take no notice of it. Or if they do, they think they will do well enough with it, and shake it off easily when they have a little leisure to attend to it. For the present they think, like #emph[Samson] with his locks cut, that they will do as at other times. Sometimes, perhaps, they complain that they are not #emph[well,] and do not know what is wrong with them; and perhaps they rise up violently in opposition to their disease. But after #emph[struggling] in vain for a while, as the vigor of their spirits and their strength fail them, they are forced to yield to the power of a #emph[consumption.] And now all they can do is barely enough to keep them alive. So it is with men brought into #emph[spiritual decays] by some secret, perplexing corruption. Perhaps they have had a vigorous principle of obedience and holiness. #emph[Indwelling sin,] watching for its opportunities, has kindled and inflamed some particular lust in them by some temptation or other. For a while, perhaps, they take little notice of it. Sometimes they #emph[complain,] but think they will do as in former times. Then, weakened in their spiritual strength without noticing it, they have work enough to do keeping alive what remains and is ready to die (Hos 5:13). I will not add anything here about preventing and forestalling this advantage of indwelling sin, since I have treated of it specially and separately elsewhere.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Fifthly,] it works by #emph[negligence in private communion with God in prayer and meditation.] I have shown before how #emph[indwelling sin] puts forth its #emph[deceitfulness] in turning the soul from watchfulness in these duties and toward them. If it prevails here, it will not fail to bring on an #emph[habitual decline] in the whole course of obedience. All neglect of private duties springs from a #emph[weariness] of God, as he complains in Isa 43:22: “But you have not called on Me, O Jacob, because you have grown weary of Me, O Israel.” Neglect of calling on God comes from weariness. And where there is #emph[weariness,] there will be withdrawing from what we are weary of. Now since God alone is the fountain and spring of spiritual life, if there is #emph[weariness] of him and withdrawing from him, it is impossible that #emph[a decay] in that life should not follow. Indeed, what men are in #emph[these duties] (I mean as to faith and love in them), that they are, and no more. Here lies the root of their obedience, and if this fails, all fruit will quickly fail. You may sometimes see a #emph[tree] flourishing with fine and pleasant leaves and fruit. After a while the #emph[leaves] begin to decay, the #emph[fruit] to wither, and the whole tree to droop. Search, and you will find that the #emph[root] has received a wound, or has perished in some way, and does not do its duty. It is by the root that the tree should draw moisture and richness from the earth to supply the trunk and branches with sap and juice for growth and fruit. So though the branches flourish for a while with what they had received, they must decay once their nourishment is cut off. So it is here. These duties of private communion with God are the means of receiving supplies of #emph[spiritual strength] from him, of sap and richness from Christ the vine and olive. While they do so, the life and course of obedience #emph[flourishes] and is fruitful, and all outward duties are cheerfully and regularly performed. But suppose there is a #emph[wound,] a defect, a failing in what should first take in the #emph[spiritual vital moisture] that should be communicated to the whole. Then the rest may keep their station and appearance for a while, but after a while profession will wither, fruits will decay, and the whole will be ready to die. That is why our Savior lets us know (Matt 6:6) that what a man is #emph[in secret,] in these private duties, that he is in the eyes of God, and no more. One reason among others is that in these duties #emph[unmixed grace] acts more vigorously than in any other duties whatever. In all or most particular duties there is the influence they may have from carnal motives, which are many, and whose ways of creeping in are subtle and imperceptible. Besides this, there is an #emph[alloy] of #emph[gifts,] which sometimes even devours the pure gold of grace that should be chief and principal in them. In these private duties there is immediate dealing between God and what is of himself in the soul. If sin once prevails by its deceits and treacheries to take the soul off from #emph[diligent attention] to communion with God and from constancy in these duties, it will not fail to bring about a #emph[decline] in the whole of a man’s obedience. It has made its entrance, and will assuredly make good its progress.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Sixthly,] growing in #emph[notions of truth without matching practice] is another thing #emph[indwelling sin] uses to bring the souls of believers into #emph[decay.] The apostle tells us that “knowledge puffs up” (1 Cor 8:1). If it is alone, not put to use in practice, it #emph[swells men] beyond a due proportion. As with a man who has #emph[dropsy,] we are not to expect that he has strength to match his size. It is like trees that are continually #emph[shooting up into growth,] which keeps them from bearing fruit. Men reach the point where they can take in and receive #emph[evangelical truths] in a new and more glorious light, or a clearer revelation than before, or new manifestations of truth they did not know before, and they please themselves in doing so. When they do this without #emph[diligent efforts] to have the power of those truths and notions on their hearts, and to have their souls conformed to them, they generally learn to treat in the same way all the truths they knew before, which were once set into their hearts with more efficacy and power. This has proved, if not the #emph[ruin,] yet the great #emph[impairing,] of many in these #emph[days of light] in which we live. By this means many have withered from humble, close #emph[walking] into an empty, barren, #emph[talking] profession. In a short time almost everything has become alike to them. Whether things were true or false, as long as they could debate them and dispute about them, all was well. This is #emph[food for sin]\; it hatches sin and increases it, and is increased by it. It is a notable way for the #emph[vanity] in the mind to exert itself without a rebuke from conscience. While men are talking, writing, and studying about religion, and hearing #emph[preaching,] perhaps with great #emph[delight,] like those in Ezek 33:32, conscience will be very well pacified, unless it is thoroughly awake and watchful, and furnished with spiritual wisdom and care. It will enter no rebukes or pleas against the way the soul is in. Yet all this may be nothing but the acting of the #emph[natural vanity] that lies in the mind, and is a principal part of the sin we are treating of. And generally it is so when men content themselves, as was said, with the notions of truth, without laboring for an experience of their power in their hearts and bringing forth their fruit in their lives. On this a #emph[decay] must follow.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Seventhly, growth in carnal wisdom] is another help to sin in producing this sad effect. “Your wisdom and knowledge,” says the prophet, “led you astray” (Isa 47:10). As much as #emph[carnal wisdom] increases, so much does faith decay. Its proper work is to teach a man #emph[to trust to himself and in himself]\; faith’s work is to trust wholly in another. So carnal wisdom labors to destroy the whole work of faith by making the soul return to a deceiving #emph[fullness] of its own. We have woeful examples of the prevailing power of this principle of decline in the days we live in. How many a poor, humble, brokenhearted creature, who followed God in simplicity and integrity of spirit, have we seen come to be dipped in a #emph[worldly, carnal frame,] and utterly wither in their profession! It came about through observing the ways and walk of others, and through giving in to the temptations to craft and cunning that their opportunities in the world gave them. Many are so #emph[stained] by it that they cannot be recognized as the men they were.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Eighthly, some great sin lying long in the heart and conscience unrepented of,] or not repented of as it ought to be and as the matter requires, furthers #emph[indwelling sin] in this work. The great turn in the life of #emph[David,] which gave his #emph[first ways] the better reputation, lay in his harboring his great sin in his conscience without fitting repentance. It was otherwise with #emph[Peter,] as we know, and he had a different outcome. A great sin will certainly give a great #emph[turn] to the life of a professor. If it is well healed in the blood of Christ, with the humbling the gospel requires, it often proves a means of more watchfulness, fruitfulness, humility, and contentment than the soul ever had before. If it is neglected, it certainly #emph[hardens the heart,] weakens spiritual strength, and enfeebles the soul, discouraging it from all communion with God, and it is a notable principle of a general decay. So #emph[David] complains (Ps 38:5): “My wounds are foul and festering because of my folly.” His present disease was not so much from his #emph[sin] as from his #emph[folly]\; not so much from the wounds he had received as from his neglect to seek their cure in time. It is like a #emph[broken bone.] If it is well set, it leaves the place stronger than before; if not, it makes the man a #emph[cripple] all his days. We only name these things briefly. Several other advantages of the same kind that #emph[sin] uses to produce this effect might also be given as instances, but these may be enough for our present purpose. Whatever it uses, sin itself is still the principle, and this is no small demonstration of its efficacy and power.]]
